lab-process-mail answers the inbox only when someone runs it. This
series makes it a one-shot program and adds lab-process-maild, which
manages it as a service under daemon(8):

    lab-process-maild start
    lab-process-maild status
    lab-process-maild stop

lab-process-mail answers the oldest inbox message and says by its exit
status whether to run it again: 0 once the inbox is empty, 75
(EX_TEMPFAIL in sysexits(3)) when it answered a message and more are
waiting, and 1 on failure. There is no daemon mode; a run by hand
gets the same statuses.

daemon(8) cannot act on its child's status, so start hands it
"lab-process-maild drain" with -R. drain runs lab-process-mail again
at once while it exits 75 and exits with any other status, so a batch
of mail is answered back to back and daemon(8) waits
lab_process_maild_interval seconds only once the inbox is empty or a
run fails. drain runs lab-process-mail in the foreground; stop sends
SIGTERM to drain, sh holds the trapped signal until the run in
progress ends, and drain then exits instead of starting another.

The rest is as in v4. The package ships lab-process-maild(8) and reads
${LOCALBASE}/etc/lab-process-maild.conf, an rc.conf(5)-style file with
four settings. daemon(8) starts from an empty environment, keeping
only HOME, USER, LOGNAME, and PATH as the operator has it, plus
lab_process_maild_env.

A stuck message is held rather than retried. lab-process-mail records
it in ~/var/db/lab-process-mail/stuck, and while it is still the
oldest in the inbox, later runs fail before the agent starts. Under
the daemon that is one failed run per interval, each costing a fetch
and no agent pass or notice, until the message is answered or
archived or the file is removed. The held message also blocks the
rest of the inbox; skipping it is fix 3 in "[BUG] lab-process-mail
gets stuck" [1], and is not part of this series.

Patch 1 makes lab-process-mail answer one message and exit. Patches 2
and 3 add the hold and the SIGTERM handling. Patch 4 adds the exit
status, and patch 5 adds the daemon with drain.

kyua test passes at every patch: 15/15, 18/18, 20/20, 20/20, and
33/33. The daemon tests run the real daemon(8) to answer three
messages back to back with a 60 second interval, to restart after a
failed run, and for status and stop; drain's loop and its SIGTERM
handling are tested on their own. mandoc -Tlint is clean.

Changes in v5:
- Add patch 4, which exits 75 while mail is waiting. Pat found one
  message per interval too slow and agreed to this contract.
- Run "lab-process-maild drain" under daemon(8) instead of
  lab-process-mail itself, and document drain in lab-process-maild(8).
- SIGTERM now reaches drain rather than lab-process-mail, so stop also
  lets a run finish that has not yet reached its pass.
